<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lyons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lyon is a city in east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>. Founded in 43 BCE as the Roman colony of Lugdunum, it became the capital of the Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and later a seat of the Burgundian kings. An imperial city within the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was annexed by France in 1312 and grew rich on banking, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving; the revolts of its silk workers in 1831 and 1834 shook the July Monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyon.jpg" alt="Lyon" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aldg692VX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyon-part-dieu-2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
